    breaks: "Insert already-sorted data into a plain BST and it silently degenerates into a slow line — your O(log n) lookups become O(n) and you won't see why until it's slow at scale. That exact failure is the reason self-balancing trees exist. Traversal bugs bite too: get the recursion wrong and you visit nodes twice, skip a subtree, or loop forever.",
    scale: "Small: any tree works. Large in memory: use a self-balancing BST so it never degenerates. Data too big for memory: that's where **B-trees** shine — wide and shallow to minimise slow disk reads, which is exactly how databases index billions of rows. Planet-scale: those B-tree indexes get replicated and sharded, but the halving-the-search idea you learn here is still doing the work.",
